      "notes": "\n  <h2>Liquids</h2>\n\n  <p>A liquid is a state of matter whose particles are close together but\n  are able to move past one another.</p>\n\n  <h3>Particle Arrangement</h3>\n\n  <p>Liquid particles are relatively close together, but their arrangement\n  is less ordered than that of particles in a solid.</p>\n\n  <h3>Particle Movement</h3>\n\n  <p>The particles can move and slide past one another.</p>\n\n  <h3>Properties of Liquids</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>They have a fixed volume.</li>\n    <li>They do not have a fixed shape.</li>\n    <li>They take the shape of their container.</li>\n    <li>They are difficult to compress.</li>\n    <li>They can flow.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Examples</h3>\n\n  <p>Water, ethanol, cooking oil and mercury are liquids at suitable\n  temperatures.</p>\n  ",
