      "notes": "\n  <h2>Particle Arrangement</h2>\n\n  <p>The particle model explains the different states of matter by considering\n  how closely particles are packed and how they are arranged.</p>\n\n  <h3>Solid</h3>\n\n  <p>Particles are very close together and arranged in relatively fixed\n  positions.</p>\n\n  <h3>Liquid</h3>\n\n  <p>Particles remain close together but are less ordered and can move past\n  one another.</p>\n\n  <h3>Gas</h3>\n\n  <p>Particles are far apart and arranged randomly.</p>\n\n  <h3>Comparison</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Solid →</strong> closest and most ordered.</p>\n  <p><strong>Liquid →</strong> close but less ordered.</p>\n  <p><strong>Gas →</strong> far apart and random.</p>\n\n  <p>The arrangement of particles helps explain properties such as shape,\n  volume and compressibility.</p>\n  ",
