      "notes": "\n  <h2>Sublimation</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>Sublimation</strong> is the change of state in which a solid\n  changes directly into a gas without passing through the liquid state.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Solid → Gas</strong></p>\n\n  <h3>What Happens to the Particles?</h3>\n\n  <p>The particles in the solid gain enough energy to escape directly into\n  the gas state.</p>\n\n  <p>The liquid state is skipped.</p>\n\n  <h3>Examples</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Solid carbon dioxide (dry ice) changes directly into carbon dioxide gas.</li>\n    <li>Iodine can form iodine vapour when heated.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Important Point</h3>\n\n  <p>Sublimation is a physical change. The chemical identity of the substance\n  remains the same.</p>\n\n  <p>For example:</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Solid CO₂ → CO₂ gas</strong></p>\n\n  <p>The substance remains carbon dioxide.</p>\n  ",
