      "notes": "<h2>Compounds</h2>\n\n  <p>A <strong>compound</strong> is a pure substance formed when two or more different elements are chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</p>\n\n  <h3>Examples</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Water — H₂O</li>\n    <li>Carbon dioxide — CO₂</li>\n    <li>Sodium chloride — NaCl</li>\n    <li>Magnesium oxide — MgO</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>The elements in a compound are chemically bonded. The compound has properties different from those of the elements that formed it.</p>\n\n  <h3>Example</h3>\n  <p>Sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine is a poisonous gas. When chemically combined, they form sodium chloride, NaCl.</p>\n\n  <h3>Key idea</h3>\n  <p><strong>Different elements + chemical combination in a fixed ratio → compound.</strong></p>\n\n  <p>Compounds can be broken down into simpler substances by chemical methods, not ordinary physical separation.</p>",
