      "notes": "<h2>Identifying Pure Substances</h2>\n\n  <p>A <strong>pure substance</strong> contains only one type of substance and has a fixed composition.</p>\n\n  <p>Pure substances are divided into two groups:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Elements</strong> — contain one type of atom.</li>\n    <li><strong>Compounds</strong> — contain two or more different elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>How to identify a pure substance</h3>\n\n  <p>Ask:</p>\n\n  <ol>\n    <li>Does it contain only one substance?</li>\n    <li>Does it have a fixed composition?</li>\n    <li>Does it have characteristic physical properties such as a definite melting point or boiling point?</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <p>If the sample is a pure substance, determine whether it is an <strong>element</strong> or a <strong>compound</strong>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Examples</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Cu → pure substance → element</li>\n    <li>O₂ → pure substance → element</li>\n    <li>H₂O → pure substance → compound</li>\n    <li>NaCl → pure substance → compound</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p><strong>Pure substance → element or compound.</strong></p>",
