      "notes": "<h2>Decantation</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>Decantation</strong> separates a liquid from a settled solid or separates immiscible liquids by carefully pouring off one component.</p>\n\n  <h3>Separating a solid from a liquid</h3>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Allow the insoluble solid to settle at the bottom.</li>\n    <li>The solid forms a sediment.</li>\n    <li>Carefully pour the liquid into another container.</li>\n    <li>Leave the sediment behind.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h3>Example</h3>\n  <p>Sand and water can be separated by allowing the sand to settle and carefully pouring off the water.</p>\n\n  <h3>Limitation</h3>\n  <p>Decantation is generally less effective than filtration when very fine solid particles remain suspended.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Key idea: settle → carefully pour off.</strong></p>",
