      "notes": "<h2>Dot-and-Cross Diagrams</h2>\n\n  <p>A <strong>dot-and-cross diagram</strong> shows the outer electrons involved in covalent bonding or electron transfer.</p>\n\n  <h3>What the symbols mean</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>A <strong>dot</strong> can represent an electron contributed by one atom.</li>\n    <li>A <strong>cross</strong> can represent an electron contributed by the other atom.</li>\n    <li>A shared pair contains one electron from each atom in a simple covalent bond.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Hydrogen chloride</h3>\n\n  <p>Hydrogen contributes one electron.</p>\n\n  <p>Chlorine contributes one electron to the shared pair.</p>\n\n  <p>The shared pair forms the H—Cl bond.</p>\n\n  <h3>Important rule</h3>\n\n  <p>When drawing a dot-and-cross diagram:</p>\n\n  <ol>\n    <li>Write the symbols of the atoms.</li>\n    <li>Determine their outer electrons.</li>\n    <li>Identify the electrons needed for stable outer levels.</li>\n    <li>Show the shared pairs.</li>\n    <li>Show remaining non-bonding electrons where required.</li>\n  </ol>",
