      "notes": "<h2>Ionic and Covalent Structures</h2>\n\n  <p>Ionic and covalent substances differ in how their particles are held together.</p>\n\n  <h3>Ionic substances</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Contain positive and negative ions.</li>\n    <li>Have strong electrostatic attractions between ions.</li>\n    <li>Form giant ionic lattices.</li>\n    <li>Conduct electricity when molten or dissolved if the ions are mobile.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Simple covalent substances</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Contain separate molecules.</li>\n    <li>Atoms within molecules are joined by covalent bonds.</li>\n    <li>Forces between molecules are generally weaker.</li>\n    <li>Usually do not conduct electricity.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Giant covalent substances</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Contain a continuous network of covalently bonded atoms.</li>\n    <li>Usually have very high melting points.</li>\n    <li>Properties depend strongly on their structure.</li>\n  </ul>",
