      "notes": "<h2>Electrodes</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>Electrodes</strong> are conductors through which electric current enters or leaves an electrolyte.</p>\n\n  <p>There are two electrodes in an electrolytic cell:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Cathode:</strong> negative electrode in electrolysis.</li>\n    <li><strong>Anode:</strong> positive electrode in electrolysis.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>Positive ions move towards the cathode.</p>\n\n  <p>Negative ions move towards the anode.</p>\n\n  <h3>Example</h3>\n\n  <p>During electrolysis of molten NaCl:</p>\n\n  <p>Na⁺ → cathode</p>\n\n  <p>Cl⁻ → anode</p>",
