      "notes": "<h2>Tetravalency of Carbon</h2>\n\n  <p>Carbon has atomic number 6 and has four electrons in its outer shell.</p>\n\n  <p>Carbon normally forms <strong>four covalent bonds</strong>. This property is called <strong>tetravalency</strong>.</p>\n\n  <p>For example, methane has the formula CH₄.</p>\n\n  <p>The carbon atom forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms.</p>\n\n  <h3>Methane</h3>\n\n  <p>CH₄ contains:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>1 carbon atom</li>\n    <li>4 hydrogen atoms</li>\n    <li>4 C–H covalent bonds</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>Carbon can also form bonds with other carbon atoms, allowing chains and rings to develop.</p>",
