      "notes": "<h2>Naming Straight-Chain Alkanes</h2>\n\n  <p>Straight-chain alkanes are named according to the number of carbon atoms in the longest continuous chain.</p>\n\n  <p>The main carbon prefixes are:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>1 carbon → meth-</li>\n    <li>2 carbons → eth-</li>\n    <li>3 carbons → prop-</li>\n    <li>4 carbons → but-</li>\n    <li>5 carbons → pent-</li>\n    <li>6 carbons → hex-</li>\n    <li>7 carbons → hept-</li>\n    <li>8 carbons → oct-</li>\n    <li>9 carbons → non-</li>\n    <li>10 carbons → dec-</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>Alkanes end with <strong>-ane</strong>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Examples</h3>\n  <p>CH₄ → methane</p>\n  <p>CH₃CH₃ → ethane</p>\n  <p>CH₃CH₂CH₃ → propane</p>\n  <p>CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ → butane</p>\n\n  <p>For a straight-chain alkane, count the carbon atoms and select the corresponding prefix, then add <strong>-ane</strong>.</p>",
