      "notes": "<h2>Strong Electrolytes</h2>\n\n  <p>A <strong>strong electrolyte</strong> is a substance that completely dissociates or ionises into ions when dissolved in water.</p>\n\n  <p>Because many ions are present and are free to move through the solution, a strong electrolyte conducts electricity well.</p>\n\n  <h3>Examples</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Hydrochloric acid, HCl</li>\n    <li>Nitric acid, HNO₃</li>\n    <li>Sodium hydroxide, NaOH</li>\n    <li>Sodium chloride, NaCl</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Example</h3>\n\n  <p>When sodium chloride dissolves:</p>\n\n  <p><strong>NaCl(aq) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>\n\n  <p>The sodium chloride produces mobile ions throughout the solution.</p>",
