      "notes": "<h2>Non-electrolytes</h2>\n\n  <p>A <strong>non-electrolyte</strong> is a substance that does not produce ions in solution and therefore does not conduct electricity through its aqueous solution.</p>\n\n  <h3>Example</h3>\n\n  <p>Sugar dissolves in water but remains mainly as neutral sugar molecules.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(s) → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq)</strong></p>\n\n  <p>The sugar molecules do not separate into ions.</p>\n\n  <p>Therefore, there are no mobile ions available to carry electrical charge through the solution.</p>\n\n  <h3>Comparison</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Strong electrolyte:</strong> completely ionises or dissociates.</li>\n    <li><strong>Weak electrolyte:</strong> partially ionises or dissociates.</li>\n    <li><strong>Non-electrolyte:</strong> does not produce ions in solution.</li>\n  </ul>",
