      "notes": "<h2>Addition Reactions</h2>\n\n  <p>Alkenes undergo addition reactions because they contain a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>\n\n  <p>In an addition reaction, atoms or groups of atoms are added across the double bond, converting the alkene into a saturated compound.</p>\n\n  <h3>Hydrogenation</h3>\n\n  <p>Hydrogen can add across the double bond of an alkene in the presence of a nickel catalyst and heat.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Ethene + Hydrogen → Ethane</strong></p>\n\n  <p>C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆</p>\n\n  <p>The C=C double bond becomes a C-C single bond.</p>\n\n  <h3>Addition of Bromine</h3>\n\n  <p>Bromine also adds across the double bond.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Ethene + Bromine → 1,2-dibromoethane</strong></p>\n\n  <p>C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂</p>\n\n  <p>This reaction is important because it provides a test for unsaturation.</p>",
