      "notes": "<h2>Crude Oil</h2>\n\n  <p>Crude oil is a naturally occurring mixture of hydrocarbons found beneath the Earth's surface.</p>\n\n  <p>It is formed from the remains of living organisms that were buried and subjected to heat and pressure over millions of years.</p>\n\n  <p>Crude oil contains many different hydrocarbons with different molecular sizes and boiling points.</p>\n\n  <h3>Why Crude Oil Is Separated</h3>\n\n  <p>The different hydrocarbons in crude oil have different uses. They must therefore be separated into groups called <strong>fractions</strong>.</p>\n\n  <p>The separation is carried out mainly by <strong>fractional distillation</strong>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Important Point</h3>\n\n  <p>Crude oil is a mixture, not a single compound. The hydrocarbons are separated physically according to differences in their boiling points.</p>",
